From 26361edef698ad567eb66a53a43ffa6d3b49f155 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yaegerbomb42 <2.03348624e+08+yaegerbomb42@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:59:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(harness): make the approval gate synchronous with
 execution (b5)

The gate was advisory: the VM's managed settings blanket-allowed Bash, Read,
Edit, Write and friends, and `claude -p` ran with no PreToolUse hook and no
permission-prompt tool, so the CLI executed every tool itself. The harness
only classified the tool_use block from stdout afterwards, opened the
approval card on a side promise chain, and checked the denial after the
child exited. `rm -rf /`, `sudo`, a force push or a read of `~/.ssh`
completed before the card rendered; a deny just relabelled the finished run.

The verdict is now synchronous:

- lib/harness/tool-gate.ts: per-run gate session. The hook's request is
  classified (allow/ask/deny), an ask waits for the user, and the answer is
  the tool's permission decision. Fail closed everywhere: no approval
  channel, unknown token, run over, timeout -> deny. Duplicate hook calls
  (image hook + --settings hook) are deduped by tool-call id, and a deny
  blocks the call rather than the run, so the model sees the reason and can
  adapt. Emits approval.required -> approval.resolved -> tool.started so the
  ordering is observable, plus tool.blocked for policy denies.
- app/api/runtime/hooks/pretooluse/route.ts: the endpoint the hook calls,
  authenticated by the per-run bearer token (it can only ask, never
  approve). Returns the Claude Code hookSpecificOutput decision.
- middleware.ts: that path authenticates itself, like the LLM proxy; without
  the bypass every hook call would 401 and deny every tool.
- lib/harness/runner.ts: opens the gate, hands the hook its URL and token
  through the exec env (never argv, and the token is redacted from
  telemetry), and keeps the stdout gate only as the fallback for a VM whose
  hook never calls home.
- lib/runtime/hook-settings.ts: a VM built from an older image gets the hook
  through a per-run --settings file, probed once per user.
- lib/harness/risk.ts: isIrreversibleExternal() so g9 full-auto can
  auto-allow in-VM asks while force pushes, publishes and deploys still ask.
- runtime + team lanes wire the gate; the hook client is seeded per run.

Tests: unit coverage for every gate path (allow, deny, ask+approve,
ask+deny, timeout, no channel, full-auto, dedupe, close, token lookup), the
route, and the real hook client spawned as a child process against a real
endpoint. The e2e test drives the real hook + route + gate + runner with a
faithful fake CLI that honours the hook and executes a tool only after it
was allowed: the target directory survives an unapproved, denied or timed
out call and disappears only after an approval.
